  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1239 PM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nBreezy conditions look to remain across portions of eastern and \nwestern NM Wednesday afternoon and Thursday, but by and large they\nare favored to be quieter weather days as weaker west to \nsouthwest flow sets up overhead. Temperatures may get up into the \nlow to mid 60s across eastern NM by Wednesday due to downsloping \nwinds, while western NM will be a day behind due to the passage of\nthe previous system.\n\nOur next system is forecast to arrive Friday and bring a return of \nwinds and precipitation. Currently, winds are favored to be the \nmore forthcoming impact, as a robust lee surface trough is \nforecast to develop across southeast CO/northeast NM. \nConcurrently, stronger 700mb winds move across the rest of the \nstate during the daytime hours, allowing for those winds to be \nfavored to mix to the surface. Overall, winds are currently \nforecast to be stronger than Tuesday's system, though still within\nthe realm of normalcy in terms of New Mexico windy days. For \nprecipitation, model guidance has gradually trended more north \nwith the trough axis, which in turn has gradually pushed \nprecipitation further into Colorado. As such, significant \naccumulation does not appear likely at this time. Mountain snow \nand low elevation rain looks to be the favored precipitation type \ncurrently.\n\nAfter the system passes, models quickly diverge in their solutions, \nwhich leads to low forecast confidence past Friday. CPC \nprecipitation outlooks continue to paint slightly favored chances \nfor above normal precipitation across at least our eastern areas \nmoving into next week. This makes sense given that even with \ndiverging model solutions, global models still paint a more active \n\"wave train\" of systems moving into western CONUS, which would favor \nus for some unsettled weather.",
